Delivered [gen_2503_01929.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2503.01929/gen_2503_01929.py). It is standard-library-only, deterministic, performs no import-time I/O, and ships the `hard` preset.

## Verification results

`selftest()` completed in 4.976 seconds:

- G1: 12/12 planted witnesses verified across all presets.
- G2: 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons.
- G3: realistic fenced response recovered all 96 shifts.
- G4: 0/200,000 random guesses succeeded; candidate-space bit length is 1,536.
- G5: 1,119,744 valid answers among 2,140,156,846,430,076,578,936,832,000 candidates at `n=4`, fraction \(5.2321\times10^{-22}\).
- G6:
  - Per-item degree/outlier attack: 0/8.
  - Largest-first greedy: 0/8.
  - 256 random restarts: 0/8.
- G7: `n=32` doubled to `n=64`; 96 items became 192, search-space bit length grew from 1,536 to 3,648, and the doubled planted witness verified.
- Final result: `all_pass = True`.

An additional 100-seed determinism/G1 sweep passed 100/100.

## LLM hardening transcript

OpenRouter was used because only `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` was present. The model was `openai/gpt-5.6-terra`; `reasoning: {"effort":"medium"}` was accepted on every call, with no fallback. The Python OpenAI SDK was not installed, so no SDK signature was assumed. The request agrees with the official [OpenAI reasoning configuration](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning).

| Preset | Seed | Solved | Result |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| easy | 8101 | Yes | verified |
| easy | 8102 | Yes | verified |
| easy | 8103 | Yes | verified |
| medium | 8201 | No | empty answer |
| medium | 8202 | No | 59 instead of 60 shifts |
| medium | 8203 | No | empty answer |
| hard | 8301 | No | 97 instead of 96 shifts |
| hard | 8302 | No | interval crossed a block boundary |
| hard | 8303 | No | 94 instead of 96 shifts |

Medium was not shipped despite 0/3 oracle solutions because its random-restart attack succeeded on 1/8 seeds. Final shipping difficulty: **hard**.

The complete raw replies are retained in the [run log](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2503.01929/codex_run.log:8772).

## Worked example

```text
Binary-lamp quotient-sum witness problem

All coordinates below are integers. A binary lamp configuration is a function
from the integers to {0,1} with only finitely many 1s. Configurations are
added pointwise modulo 2 (XOR).

There are 6 labelled interval configurations. Item i initially has value 1
at coordinates 0 through length_i-1. A shift s_i moves it to the CLOSED
interval [s_i, s_i + length_i - 1].

Target CLOSED blocks:
[0, 47], [49, 96]

Target block length T: 48
Item lengths:
1: 21
2: 18
3: 16
4: 14
5: 14
6: 13

Give the ordered shifts inside <answer></answer> tags.
```

```text
<answer>[14, 63, 81, 49, 0, 35]</answer>
```

```python
verify(inst, [14, 63, 81, 49, 0, 35])
# (True, "ok")

verify(inst, [14, 63, 81, 49, 0])
# (False, "wrong_length_expected_6_got_5")
```

## NOTES

The full paper’s Section 3.2 supplies the exact quotient-sum definition. Proposition 4.2 gives the rank-zero interval-lamp reduction from 3-PARTITION, and Theorem 4.3 proves NP-hardness for nontrivial \(A\) and infinite \(B\). The generator fixes \(A=\mathbb Z_2\), \(B=\mathbb Z\), and \(h=0\), normalizing the target’s translation. [Full paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.01929)

The easy regimes explicitly avoided are finite \(B\) (Theorem 4.4), \(h\geq\operatorname{rank}(B)\) (Theorem 4.20), one function (Theorem 4.23), bounded function count (Theorem 4.24), large genus (Corollary 5.4), and bounded conjugate count (Corollary 5.6).

The complete numeric witness is sampled before public lengths are derived. Item labels are uniformly shuffled, and all three within-block gaps use the same symmetric rejection distribution. Hardness comes from crowded accidental sum-triples and scale, not distinguishable plant/decoy distributions.
