 Corollary 5.3 gives a very limited picture
for orientable equations.
For 𝐍𝐏 {\mathbf{NP}} -hardness it employs spherical equations only
and neglects other types of orientable equations.
Below we analyze other cases with the goal
to identify the reasons for computational hardness. 
 
 
 5.1.1. Non-uniform problem: genus g g 

 
 The value of the genus g g of a given equation ℰ {\mathcal{E}} 
relative to the rank of the group B B directly affects the hardness of solving ℰ {\mathcal{E}} . 
 
 
 Corollary 5.4 . 
 
 The Diophantine problem for the class of orientable quadratic
equations ( 2 )
with genus satisfying g ≥ rank ​ ( B ) / 2 g\geq\text{rank}(B)/2 
is in 𝐏 {\mathbf{P}} . 
 
 
 
 Proof. 
 
 Proposition 3.2 reduces
orientable quadratic equations ( 2 ) in A ≀ B A\wr B 
